fund-java/17-spring-ioc-and-dependency-i.explanation:
Keep registrations per type in order, cache singleton instances per registration, and apply the candidate rules in `get`:

```java
var candidates = regs.getOrDefault(type, List.of());
if (candidates.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException("no bean of type " + type.getSimpleName());
if (candidates.size() == 1) return type.cast(instance(candidates.get(0)));
var primaries = candidates.stream().filter(r -> r.primary).toList();
if (primaries.size() != 1) throw new IllegalStateException("expected single matching bean but found " + candidates.size());
return type.cast(instance(primaries.get(0)));
```

`instance` returns the cached object for a singleton (creating it on first use) and calls the factory every time for a prototype. Passing `this` into the factory is how a bean resolves its own dependencies, the way constructor injection does.

Returning the first registration instead of applying the primary rule fails "several candidates without one primary fail", because nothing is thrown. Checking only `primaries.isEmpty()` rather than `size() != 1` lets the first of two primaries win, and "several candidates without one primary fail" fails on its two-primary case. Count the candidates in the message, not the primaries: a message saying `found 0` fails "several candidates without one primary fail" as well. Building singletons inside `register` instead of on first use fails "a singleton is created on first use, once". (Spring itself creates singletons eagerly at startup unless a bean is marked `@Lazy`; this drill's rule is lazy.)

